## The mineral springs of Marienbad

Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) stands on unusual ground: more than forty mineral springs rise within the town, and roughly another hundred in its surroundings. They are cold acidulous waters at 7 to 10 °C all year round, carbonated by natural carbon dioxide of volcanic origin — which sets them fundamentally apart from the hot thermal springs of nearby Karlovy Vary. Each spring passes through different layers of rock and therefore has a different composition: some are strongly mineralised and bitter-salty, others mild and almost neutral in taste.

It is precisely this variety that explains why the **drinking cure** — drinking prescribed mineral water directly at the spring — has been practised in Marienbad for over two centuries, and why it is always drawn up by a spa physician. This page explains how the waters differ, how the drinking cure works and for which diagnoses it forms part of treatment. The location, access, opening hours, taste and temperature of the individual springs are in the [springs overview](/en/springs-overview).

{% youtube videoId="81MdKQ99QTo" title="5 stars of our treatments — natural therapy with tradition" /%}

## Why the waters differ

{% figure
   src="/images/library/springs/domed-spring-pavilion.jpg"
   alt="White domed pavilion over a spring with colonnaded wings and a bronze fountain"
   caption="The domed spring pavilion frames a modern bronze fountain beneath a bright blue sky."
   width="wide" /%}

Balneology classifies mineral waters by their predominant dissolved substances. In Marienbad several types occur side by side, and that matters for the drinking cure: one water is prescribed for digestive complaints, another for diseases of the urinary tract.

- **Carbon dioxide.** Common to all the local springs — hence the name acidulous waters. It gives the water its sparkle and fresh taste; at high concentration it rises at the Maria Spring as an almost pure gas, which the spa uses in [CO₂ therapy](/en/co2-therapy).
- **Sulphates and sodium (Glauber's salt).** The characteristic component of the **Cross Spring** and the **Ferdinand Spring**, the town's two most strongly mineralised waters. Sodium sulphate, or Glauber's salt, gives the water a bitter taste and a laxative effect; these springs are traditionally prescribed for diseases of the digestive system and metabolic disorders.
- **Bicarbonates, calcium and magnesium.** The **Rudolph Spring** is rich in calcium, the **Caroline Spring** in magnesium. Both waters are traditionally prescribed for diseases of the kidneys and urinary tract; the Rudolph Spring also features in drinking cures for osteoporosis.
- **Iron.** The **Ambrose Spring** and the forest springs in the surroundings (Farská Kyselka, Balbín Spring) contain iron, which leaves a typical rust-coloured deposit at the outlet. In the spa tradition, iron-bearing waters were prescribed for anaemia; today the physician decides on their inclusion according to the blood count.
- **Mildly mineralised alkaline waters.** The **Forest Spring** is the mildest of the great drinking springs. It is traditionally used for diseases of the upper respiratory tract — including inhalation and gargling — and for stomach complaints.

A detailed profile of each spring, its location and taste is given in the [springs overview](/en/springs-overview); the history and curiosities of the individual outlets in the [guide to the mineral springs](/en/magazine/mineral-springs-complete-guide).

## How the drinking cure works

{% treatment-box title="Drinking cure" description="Drinking mineral water directly at the spring in the quantity, at the time of day and at the temperature prescribed by the spa physician after the initial examination. The water is drunk slowly, in sips, usually on an empty stomach before a meal and while walking slowly. Which spring, how many cups and when is set by the medical schedule, which is adjusted during the stay according to check-up examinations." icon="water" /%}

The drinking cure is the oldest and simplest way in which the Marienbad springs are used. Its form has changed little in two centuries:

1. **Initial examination.** The spa physician assesses the diagnosis, other illnesses, medicines taken and the examination results the patient brings along, and draws up an individual schedule: the spring or combination of springs, the number of cups, the time of day and the interval before meals.
2. **Morning cure.** The main part of the drinking cure takes place in the morning on an empty stomach. The guest goes with a porcelain spa cup to the pavilion of the prescribed spring, fills it and drinks the water slowly, in small sips, while walking along the colonnade or through the park. The walk is part of the procedure, not just its backdrop.
3. **Further doses during the day.** According to the schedule, drinking is repeated before lunch and possibly in the afternoon, often from a different spring than in the morning. Water from different springs is not combined at will — each has a different composition.
4. **Check-ups and adjustments.** During the stay the physician adjusts the schedule according to how the patient tolerates the water and what the check-up examinations show.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/drinking-cure/spa-cup-colonnade-pour.jpg"
   alt="A hand holds a porcelain spa cup under a mineral water outlet at a colonnade pillar"
   caption="The classic spa cup fills with mineral water beneath the colonnade's columns."
   width="wide" /%}

The water from the springs is cold (7–10 °C); the temperature at which it should be drunk is, like the quantity, part of the medical prescription. Besides drinking, mineral water from the Forest Spring is also used for **inhalations** and gargling in diseases of the respiratory tract — these procedures are described on the [climate therapy](/en/climate-therapy) page.

The drinking cure lasts for the whole of the treatment stay. Its length is determined not by the procedure but by the item of the Czech indication list the patient arrives with: in comprehensive spa care it is 21 or 28 days depending on the indication, in contributory care 14 or 21 days; self-paying guests choose the length in consultation with the spa physician. Practical advice — how to handle the cup, what to bring and what a spa day looks like — is summarised in the [guide to the drinking cure](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide).

{% youtube videoId="OujefkpfnVY" title="The drinking cure — Rudolph Spring" /%}

## Why the physician decides which spring

The Marienbad springs are not table water. Strongly mineralised waters with Glauber's salt have a laxative effect and contain a considerable amount of sodium; bicarbonate waters change the pH of the urine. In a correctly chosen patient that is the goal of treatment — in another patient it may be undesirable. A review of drinking cures in digestive and biliary diseases concludes that the type of water determines which indication it is used for, and that dose and timing are set by the physician ([Dumitrescu et al., 2021](https://doi.org/10.3892/etm.2021.11045), *Exp Ther Med*).

Hence the following applies:

- **Who chooses the spring.** Whether the drinking cure is included in the treatment plan, and the choice of spring, daily quantity and timing, is decided by the spa physician after the initial examination — for patients with urinary stones only after analysis of the stone and urine, and for patients with heart disease, kidney disease or high blood pressure in cooperation with their treating doctor.
- **When to seek advice beforehand.** If you have a prescribed fluid or salt restriction (for example in heart or kidney failure), take diuretics or suffer from recurrent diarrhoea, consult the spa physician before starting the drinking cure and do not drink mineral water in larger quantities without their schedule.
- **General contraindications.** The general contraindications of spa rehabilitation care under the Czech indication list apply to the drinking cure — among others all diseases in the acute stage, clinical signs of circulatory failure, infectious diseases transmissible from person to person and pregnancy. The complete list is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

For a healthy visitor, freely tasting a cup of water at the colonnade is an ordinary part of a spa walk. A therapeutic drinking cure — a prescribed quantity every day for the length of the stay — is something else and belongs in the hands of a physician.

## Which conditions the drinking cure is used for

The drinking cure is not a separate item of the Czech indication list (Decree No. 2/2015 Coll.); it is a procedure the spa physician builds into the treatment plan for the diagnosis the patient arrives with. In Marienbad it forms the basis of the programme above all for three indication groups.

**Diseases of the digestive system (group III).** The traditional domain of the Cross Spring and the Ferdinand Spring. Select your diagnosis to see which type of care and length of stay the list assigns to it:

{% indication-picker group="digestive" title="Diseases of the digestive system in the indication list" /%}

**Diseases of the urinary system (group VIII).** The domain of the bicarbonate waters with calcium and magnesium — the Rudolph and Caroline Springs. More in the article on [treatment of the kidneys and urinary tract](/en/magazine/kidney-urinary-treatment).

{% indication-picker group="urinary" title="Diseases of the urinary system in the indication list" /%}

**Metabolic disorders (group IV).** For diabetes and other metabolic diagnoses, the drinking cure from the Cross Spring is part of a programme centred on supervised exercise, diet and education; what such a stay looks like is described in the article on [spa treatment and lifestyle diseases](/en/magazine/civilization-diseases-spa).

{% indication-picker group="metabolic" title="Metabolic disorders in the indication list" /%}

For **diseases of the respiratory system (group V)**, mineral water from the Forest Spring is used for inhalations and gargling as a supplement to respiratory rehabilitation and climate therapy; the list of diagnoses is on the page [respiratory system in the indication list](/en/indications-and-contraindications). The complete overview of all indication groups treated in Marienbad is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

## What a patient can realistically expect

The drinking cure has a measurable effect where the composition of the water matches the complaint. What controlled studies and guidelines have documented:

- **Sulphate waters and intestinal transit.** In a double-blind randomised trial of 226 patients with functional constipation, 50% of patients responded to a sulphate-magnesium mineral water after 14 days, compared with 29% with the control water ([Dupont et al., 2019](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nut.2019.02.018), *Nutrition*). This was a French water of different composition and the study was funded by its producer; it does, however, show the principle behind the traditional use of the Cross Spring — sulphates have a measurable effect on the bowel.
- **Bicarbonate waters and urine composition.** In a double-blind crossover trial in 34 patients with recurrent calcium oxalate stones, a bicarbonate water raised urinary pH and the excretion of citrate and magnesium, substances that inhibit crystal formation; calcium oxalate supersaturation fell with both waters tested thanks to the larger volume, while the risk of phosphate stones rose ([Karagülle et al., 2007](https://consensus.app/papers/details/382178a2b7565f0da7a6bc61a1723e47/), *World J Urol*). The follow-up lasted only three days — and it is precisely this double effect that is the reason the spring is chosen only after analysis of the stone.
- **Fluid volume.** According to the European Association of Urology guideline, adequate fluid intake is the basis of preventing recurrent urinary stones, together with a metabolic work-up and treatment targeted at the stone type ([Skolarikos et al., 2024](https://consensus.app/papers/details/e104f08c54a75b18b24c5090e527a34a/), *Eur Urol*). The drinking cure is a way to acquire a regular drinking routine under supervision and take it home.

For metabolic diagnoses and diseases of the respiratory tract, the drinking cure and inhalations are used on the basis of long spa practice as part of a broader programme drawn up by the spa physician. How the drinking cure affects you is assessed by the physician during your stay on the basis of check-up examinations; the long-term follow-up of urological and nephrological patients in Marienbad is the subject of an [ongoing clinical study](/en/magazine/kidney-urinary-treatment).

## Further procedures from natural healing resources

The springs are one of Marienbad's four natural healing resources. Alongside the drinking cure, the spa physician builds procedures from the other three into the treatment plan:

- **[CO₂ therapy](/en/co2-therapy)** — dry gas baths, carbonated mineral baths and gas injections with natural carbon dioxide from the Maria Spring, used above all for diseases of the circulatory system.
- **[Peloid therapy](/en/peloid-therapy)** — wraps, packs and baths with peat from the Slavkov Forest, used above all for diseases of the musculoskeletal system.
- **[Climate therapy](/en/climate-therapy)** — the terrain cure, outdoor breathing exercises and inhalations in a sub-montane climate at an altitude of 630 metres.

