Which procedures a patient receives, and how many, is set by the spa physician at the initial examination; the individual pages describe how the procedures work, which indications they are used for and when they are not suitable.

## A spa tradition since 1818

The springs were known to the inhabitants of the surrounding villages and to the monks of the nearby Teplá monastery long before the spa was founded. Systematic medical use began only at the close of the 18th century: the monastery physician Josef Jan Nehr described the springs, began prescribing them to patients and pushed through the building of the first spa houses. In 1818 Mariánské Lázně was granted the status of a public spa.

Rapid growth followed: spa houses, hotels, colonnades and pavilions over the springs were built, and Marienbad took its place among the spas most sought after by the European nobility. Its guests included monarchs, poets and composers — the most famous of them remains Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, who visited the spa repeatedly between 1820 and 1823 and credited the local water more than all his doctors.

This tradition survived even the turbulent 20th century. Today Marienbad combines the spa tradition with the methods of contemporary medicine: the drinking cure remains the foundation, but it is prescribed on the basis of an examination and within a treatment plan that also includes exercise, diet and other procedures. More about the town's past is on the [history](/en/history) page.

## Modern spa facilities

The traditional spa houses of Marienbad have been sensitively modernised in recent years without losing their historic character. The Ensana spa hotels, such as Ensana Nové Lázně, combine classic spa architecture with contemporary comfort — guests bathe here in the restored Roman Baths of 1896, once frequented by the British King Edward VII.

Besides the drinking cure and procedures from natural resources, the spa facilities offer physiotherapy, therapeutic exercise, electrotherapy, inhalations and underwater massage. The spa physicians draw up an individual treatment plan for every guest on the basis of the initial examination and adjust it during the stay.

For guests who are looking for rest rather than a treatment cure, there are shorter wellness stays; they are regenerative, not therapeutic, in character. An overview of the hotels and their focus is on the [accommodation](/en/accommodation) page.

## Frequently asked questions

### Can the springs be visited freely and the water drunk?

Yes, the main springs in the colonnades and pavilions of the town centre are open to the public and tasting the water is free. Opening hours and locations are in the [springs overview](/en/springs-overview). A therapeutic drinking cure — regular drinking of a prescribed quantity — is drawn up by the spa physician.

### Which spring should I drink?

That depends on your diagnosis, other illnesses and the medicines you take. After the initial examination the spa physician draws up a schedule for you: which spring, how many cups and at what time of day.

### How long does the drinking cure last?

For the whole of the treatment stay. Its length is determined by the item of the Czech indication list you arrive with — in comprehensive care 21 or 28 days, in contributory care 14 or 21 days; self-paying guests agree it with the spa physician.

### Can I drink mineral water if my fluid or salt intake is restricted?

Consult the spa physician beforehand. Strongly mineralised springs contain a considerable amount of sodium, and others change the pH of the urine; with a fluid restriction, heart or kidney failure or when taking diuretics, the physician decides whether and in what quantity the drinking cure is suitable.

### Is the drinking cure covered by health insurance?

For guests insured in the Czech Republic, the drinking cure forms part of the treatment within both comprehensive and contributory spa care, provided your diagnosis is in the indication list and the insurer has approved the stay on the basis of the treating doctor's proposal. Which diagnoses qualify is set out on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

